
# Remove setjmp longjmp from error handling code
This is used internally to throw parsing errors to the top level parse functions.
Setting the handler for each call in a pull parse seems excessive so 
test to see if it would be 'better' to return errors. 

# Assert arguments
Although the code base is well tested it would be clearer, and safer, to
assert what we can about expected argument values.

# Return single item from generator
At the moment the user can request a c string or a length and byte array,
and the memory for both is managed in the generator so the string is 
invalid once the generator is freed.
Use a structure with length and flexible array member holding null terminated
string.  This memory would be the responsibility of the caller to free.
Deserialization (see below) would look better with this in place.

# Separate out the Allocator code
It will be needed by the ser/deser code (see below) so it might be cleaner if
it were a separate entity.  We do not want serde to use json parser internals.

# Create proof of concept serde attributes
Use C23 scoped attributes to generate JSON serialization/deserialization
support similar in sprit to Rust serde/json.
Ensure it works across main compilers (see below)

# Expand compiler / platform support
GCC, Clang, MS VSCode
Linux 64, Win 64, ?xxBSD
x86_64, ?Arm, ?other 

# SIMD support
Investigate how to leverage simd whilst still supporting different flavours
of Json.  Comments are especially challenging as they conflict with the string
parsing in simdjson.  Comments can contain double quotes and backslashes and 
strings can contain comment characters.

# Investigate and improve use of compiler flags
Which settings would be most useful?
How do they vary across compilers?
